<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorraine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorraine is a region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, whose name derives from Lotharingia, the share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> after the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. It was long contested between French and German rulers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> annexed part of Lorraine together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> in 1871, and France regained it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorraine.jpg" alt="Lorraine" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niko67000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_Lorraine_2018.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles the Bold sought to link his lands and win a royal crown, but his wars against the Swiss Confederates and <b>Lorraine</b> ended with his death at the Battle of Nancy in January 1477.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lorraine</b> takes its name from Lotharingia, the middle kingdom created for Lothar II at the partition of the Carolingian empire in 855, which stretched from Frisia to the Jura.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Lorraine</b> reforms revived it, but it could not resist revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The finest works are in metal: the bronze flagons from Basse-Yutz in <b>Lorraine</b>, inlaid with coral and enamel; the gold-covered helmet from Agris; the Battersea Shield, the Witham Shield and the horned Waterloo Helmet from British rivers; bronze mirrors engraved with spirals; and gold torcs, the neck rings…</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>In the German Empire that followed, which included Alsace–<b>Lorraine</b>, it stood beside &quot;Heil dir im Siegerkranz&quot; as an unofficial second national anthem, and Bismarck granted the composer and the author's family an annual pension.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther King Jr. was shot dead on the balcony of the <b>Lorraine</b> Motel, where he had come to support striking sanitation workers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
